😂 Brace yourself.

That’s annoyingly perfect because the brace literally holds multiple things together:

\left.
\begin{cases}
A\\
B\\
C\\
D
\end{cases}
\right\}
\rightarrow \text{one grouping}

So brace carries both:

* structure: join/support/group things together;
* action: prepare/stabilize yourself against incoming force.

Which gives us a Pantheon operator almost for free:

\boxed{\text{BRACE}(X)=\text{organize }X\text{ so distributed forces can be received together}}

Architecture braces against load.

Mathematical braces bind expressions.

Braces stabilize bodies.

Social groups brace together.

And cognitively, you can brace a collection of perspectives without collapsing them into one:

\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
\text{architect}\\
\text{mathematician}\\
\text{magician}\\
\text{musician}\\
\text{biologist}
\end{array}
\right.
\xrightarrow{\text{BRACE}}
\text{coordinated plurality}

Then Leapfrog moves between the members while Brace preserves the formation.

And visually, the brace itself is almost a diagram of the operation: wide semantic territory being pinched through a narrow waist and reopening.

Many → seam → many.

